        <div id="should-you-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Should You Seal Brick at All?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Often you should not, at least not yet. Good-quality face brick is fired clay and holds up well by itself; the weak points in most walls are the mortar joints, flashing, caps and gutters, not the bricks. When a wall is getting wet, find the reason first: gutters spilling over, missing drip edges, failed caulk around windows or worn-out mortar. Sealing a wall with any of those faults treats the symptom and leaves the cause in place.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What signs mean brick needs a sealer?</h3>

          <p>
            Sealing is worth doing when the brick itself soaks up too much water. Watch for brick that stays dark for hours after rain, white efflorescence salts, spalling where faces are popping off, damp patches on the inside face of solid masonry walls, and moss or algae on shaded faces. Soft historic brick, sand-molded brick and walls with heavy weather exposure are the usual candidates.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you test brick for water absorption?</h3>

          <p>
            Use a garden hose. Spray one section of wall for a minute and watch. If the water runs off and the brick returns to its dry color within a few minutes, absorption is modest and sealing is optional. If the brick drinks the water in and stays dark for an hour, it is taking on more moisture than is healthy, which matters most heading into winter. Try more than one wall, because a face that takes the prevailing weather often behaves differently from a sheltered one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does the mortar need repointing before you seal?</h3>

          <p>
            Check it before you buy any sealer. Joints recessed by more than about a quarter inch, or mortar that crumbles when you scrape it with a key, need repointing first. Sealer will not hold in failing mortar, and repointing is the repair that actually stops water at the joints.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="breathability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Does Brick Sealer Need to Be Breathable?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because water reaches a brick wall from more directions than the face, and the wall needs a way to dry. Rain lands on the outside, moisture wicks up from the ground, condensation forms inside the wall and leaks start at the top. A healthy wall copes by drying out, mostly through its face. A vapor-permeable sealer keeps that drying path open; a non-breathable film shuts it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What happens when moisture gets trapped in brick?</h3>

          <p>
            Where winters freeze and thaw, trapped water does real harm. Water in the outer layer of brick expands by roughly 9 percent as it freezes, and cycle after cycle breaks away the face of the brick, the failure called spalling. A glossy acrylic or a heavy paint on exterior brick can collect moisture right behind the film and make spalling worse.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do penetrating repellents keep brick breathable?</h3>

          <p>
            Silane and siloxane repellents line the pores with a water-repellent layer without plugging them. Liquid water beads and runs away while vapor keeps moving outward. Our <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealer guide</a> follows the same logic for slabs. It is also why masonry restoration guidance from organizations such as the Brick Industry Association prefers breathable clear repellents over films whenever a treatment is needed at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Silane, siloxane or a blend?</h3>

          <p>
            Silanes reach deepest into dense material, siloxanes perform well on more porous surfaces, and blends cover both, a good match for brick walls where the brick and the mortar absorb at different rates. Our explainer on <a href="/silane-vs-siloxane-sealers/">silane vs siloxane sealers</a> goes further into how the two chemistries differ. The table below compares the sealer types you will find on the shelf.
          </p>

