        <div id="coverage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Much Brick Sealer Do You Need, and What Does It Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Porosity decides almost everything. A dense, hard-fired face brick may spread a gallon over 150 to 250 square feet, while soft or sand-molded brick may take a gallon for every 75 to 125 square feet. Labels quote a range, so seal a measured section first, work out how much you actually used, and only then buy for the whole house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you measure a brick wall?</h3>

          <p>
            Multiply length by height for each wall face and subtract large openings such as garage doors. A typical two-story brick house can carry 2,000 square feet or more of brick, which means 10 to 20 gallons or more for one coat on porous brick. A chimney might be 100 to 200 square feet, and a patio is simply its floor area.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much does brick sealer cost per gallon?</h3>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, big-box multi-surface products run roughly $25 to $50 per gallon, mid-range masonry repellents $50 to $100, and premium concentrated silane-siloxanes $90 to $150. Premium products often cover more area per gallon, which narrows the gap.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does professional brick sealing cost?</h3>

          <p>
            Typically $1 to $3 per square foot, and more when the job includes cleaning, repointing or work at height. A single-story wall you can reach from a ladder is a very manageable DIY job. Two-story walls, chimneys and steep roofs are where a contractor with staging and fall protection is worth paying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Long Does Brick Sealer Last?
          </h2>

          <p>
            On walls, penetrating silane-siloxane repellents commonly carry life claims of 5 to 10 years, and some premium products claim more. Horizontal surfaces such as patios and wall caps wear faster from foot traffic, standing water and sun, and often need attention every 3 to 5 years.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you know when brick needs resealing?</h3>

          <p>
            Repeat the hose test from section 3; it doubles as the maintenance check. When water stops beading and the brick darkens quickly and stays dark, the repellent is fading. Penetrating sealers never need stripping: clean the wall and apply another coat the same way as before.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why do film sealers need more upkeep?</h3>

          <p>
            A worn film looks patchy and may peel. Recoating over a degraded film gives uneven results, and the old layer often has to come off with a chemical stripper first, which is messy work on textured brick.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What makes a brick sealer last longer?</h3>

          <p>
            Between treatments, keep gutters clean, maintain the caulk at windows and penetrations, and repair mortar as soon as joints begin to erode. Those details keep more water off and out of the wall than any sealer, and each coat of repellent lasts longer because of them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="patios" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. How Do You Seal a Brick Patio, Walkway or Steps?
          </h2>

          <p>
            With a penetrating repellent rated for horizontal surfaces, because flat brick has a harder life than a wall. Rain sits on it instead of running off, foot traffic wears the surface, leaves and planters leave tannin and rust stains, and in cold regions it may get de-icing salt. Brick pavers set in sand also have joints that wash out and sprout weeds, which a sealer only partly solves.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What works best on mortared brick patios and steps?</h3>

          <p>
            A penetrating repellent rated for horizontal surfaces is the safest choice. It stops the brick soaking up water, reduces freeze damage and leaves traction unchanged. If the brick has faded, an enhancing penetrating product such as Ghostshield 8510 restores color without the slick surface of a gloss film.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you seal brick pavers set in sand?</h3>

          <p>
            You have two goals: protect the brick and lock the joints. Some paver sealers are designed to harden joint sand as well as repel water. Refill the joints first, preferably with <a href="/best-polymeric-sand/">polymeric sand</a> where it is appropriate, then seal according to the product directions once everything is dry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is a wet-look sealer safe on a brick walkway?</h3>

          <p>
            Not a high-gloss acrylic film. On walkways and pool decks it looks striking on a dry day but can become slippery when wet, and it wears unevenly along traffic paths, leaving patchy, dull lanes. For a wet look on a patio, choose a breathable enhancing repellent and accept a softer sheen.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="retaining-walls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. Should You Seal a Brick Retaining Wall, Garden Wall or Foundation?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A retaining wall can gain more from sealing than almost any other brick structure, and it can also be ruined faster by the wrong product. Soil behind the wall keeps the back of the brick damp, so water is always moving through toward the exposed face. A film on that face traps the water, which in cold climates all but guarantees spalling and efflorescence.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you protect a brick retaining wall?</h3>

          <p>
            Drainage comes first: a gravel backfill zone, a perforated drain at the base and weep holes that let water escape. If the exposed face still absorbs heavily after that, treat it with a breathable penetrating repellent. Do not seal the back face, and do not try to waterproof the wall from the front with coatings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you protect a freestanding garden wall?</h3>

          <p>
            Garden walls get wet from the top. A sound coping or cap, with a drip edge that throws water clear of the face, does most of the work. Seal the cap and the top courses generously, since they take the most water, and check the cap joints regularly, because a crack there sends water straight down into the core.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you use a brick sealer on a basement or foundation wall?</h3>

          <p>
            Not a clear above-grade repellent. Below-grade brick foundations and basement walls face different problems, mainly hydrostatic pressure and wicking from the soil, which those products are not designed for. For walls that leak into a basement, our <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/">basement wall sealer guide</a> covers products made for below-grade masonry.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="interior-brick" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. Should You Seal Interior Exposed Brick?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Only for looks and practicality, because brick inside a home rarely needs water protection. Old brick can shed sand and dust, soak up cooking grease in a kitchen and look dry or chalky. A clear sealer binds the loose particles, makes the wall easier to wipe down and can deepen the color.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which sealer suits interior brick?</h3>

          <p>
            A film-forming product such as Seal-Krete Clear-Seal is a reasonable choice indoors, where the wall never sees rain or freeze-thaw. Choose matte or satin for a natural look, since gloss can look plastic on rough brick. Apply thin coats with a roller or brush, working the sealer into the mortar joints.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you prep an interior brick wall?</h3>

          <p>
            Vacuum it with a brush attachment, then wipe it with a damp cloth to pick up the remaining dust, and let it dry fully. If the brick was recently exposed by removing plaster, look for leftover plaster or adhesive, which can show through as blotches once sealed, and clean it off first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why test before sealing indoor brick?</h3>

          <p>
            Some old brick darkens dramatically under any sealer, and trying it on a few bricks in a hidden corner is the only way to know whether you like the result. Ventilate the room well while the sealer cures, even with a water-based product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Common Mistakes With Brick Sealer
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a glossy film on exterior brick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acrylic and polyurethane films look good on day one, then hold moisture inside exterior brick, worst of all where winters freeze and thaw. Within a few winters that can mean peeling film, cloudy patches and spalled brick faces. Outdoors, use a breathable penetrating repellent, and save film-formers for interior or covered brick.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over efflorescence</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                White salt deposits are a sign the wall is moving water. Seal over them and the salts can be trapped just beneath the surface, where crystals forming in the pores break off the brick face. Clean the deposits, deal with the moisture source, and wait until the wall stays clean through a dry spell before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping mortar repairs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most water gets into a wall through the mortar rather than the brick, and no sealer can fill eroded or cracked joints. Repoint failing joints first and give the fresh mortar the full cure time its maker specifies. Sealing a wall with open joints wastes product and leaves every leak path exactly where it was.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying too little product</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A penetrating repellent protects by depth. A light misting looks finished but treats only a shallow layer that wears away quickly. Flood the brick until sealer runs down several inches below the spray, and add a wet-on-wet second coat when the label calls for one. Thirsty brick needs more than the label minimum.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blasting brick with high pressure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An aggressive pressure wash wears down mortar and strips the fired skin off softer brick, leaving a rougher, thirstier surface than you started with. Clean with a masonry cleaner and a stiff brush, keep any pressure low and the nozzle a safe distance back, and let the wall dry completely before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            15. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a product sold for concrete work on a brick wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the type. Penetrating silane-siloxane sealers are often labeled for concrete, brick, block and pavers alike, and those suit brick. Film-forming concrete sealers, such as high-gloss acrylics made for driveways, limit breathability and are a poor match for exterior brick walls. Before putting a concrete product on a wall, confirm the label names brick and calls the product vapor permeable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a realistic reapplication schedule for brick?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typical manufacturer claims put walls treated with a quality penetrating repellent at 5 to 10 years between coats, with patios and wall caps due sooner. Run the hose test every year or two: when water soaks in and the brick stays dark, it is time to reapply. Film-forming sealers need recoating more often, in some cases every one to three years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a clear repellent darken or shine brick?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A standard penetrating repellent is meant to leave brick looking unchanged once it dries, although the wall can look darker for a few hours while still wet. Enhancing products such as Ghostshield 8510 darken it on purpose, and acrylic films add a sheen and deepen the color. Try any product on a hidden spot and judge it fully dry before sealing the whole wall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a repellent cure white salt deposits on a wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on its own. It can cut future efflorescence by limiting how much water gets into the brick, but it leaves existing deposits in place and cannot stop salts driven by water from behind or below the wall. Clean off the deposits, repair gutters, grading and flashing, and seal only once the wall stays dry. Sealing over active salts can damage the brick.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to treat brick around a fireplace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, on the surround and hearth, using a product rated for fireplace use; that is often a film-forming sealer that controls dust and adds a soft sheen. Keep every sealer out of the firebox itself, which reaches temperatures most sealers are not designed to handle. Let the fireplace sit cold for at least a day before you apply anything.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should historic or soft brick be treated?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only with care. Old, soft brick and lime mortar depend on breathing more than modern brick does, so if sealing is truly needed, use only a highly breathable penetrating repellent and test it first. Many preservation specialists advise against sealing historic masonry unless repairs alone cannot solve a specific water problem. A non-breathable film on historic brick can do serious damage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is painting brick a good alternative?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, but paint is a long-term commitment that changes how the wall handles moisture. If you go ahead, choose a breathable masonry paint or a mineral silicate paint instead of ordinary exterior latex, and paint only brick that is dry and free of efflorescence. When the only goal is keeping water out, a clear penetrating repellent is the less invasive choice.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

